\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{Geometry comes back as WKB by default.}
\code{dbConnect(geometry = )} chooses: \code{"blob"}, the default set in \href{https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-r/blob/main/R/dbConnect__duckdb_driver.R}{\code{R/dbConnect__duckdb_driver.R}},
returns raw vectors; \code{"wk"} returns \code{wk_wkb}, which \code{sf::st_as_sfc()} converts onward.
\href{https://duckdb.org/docs/current/sql/data_types/geometry}{\code{GEOMETRY}} is a core DuckDB type since 1.5,
so reading one needs no extension,
but the geometry functions are still \code{spatial}'s, and so is the CRS provider that resolves a name like \code{EPSG:4326}
(\href{https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-r/blob/main/handbook/usage/extensions/README.md}{\verb{extensions/}}).
\item \strong{The column's CRS reaches R either way.}
It is an attribute on \code{wk_wkb}, and PROJJSON in the metadata of the \code{geoarrow.wkb} field an Arrow result carries:
the engine registers that Arrow extension type in both directions, so geometry and CRS also round-trip through Arrow.
\item \strong{Writing a geometry means writing WKT, not WKB.}
A \code{character} column of \code{sf::st_as_text()} output with \code{field.types = c(geom = "GEOMETRY")} lands a \code{GEOMETRY} column in one statement,
because the \code{VARCHAR} cast parses WKT;
spell the CRS into the type, as \code{"GEOMETRY('EPSG:4267')"}, to keep it, which needs \code{spatial} loaded.
WKB has no such cast:
\code{BLOB} to \code{GEOMETRY} is unimplemented, so the same call over \code{sf::st_as_binary()} output fails,
and so do \code{dbAppendTable()} and a parameter bound to a \code{GEOMETRY} cast.
\code{ST_GeomFromWKB()} does the conversion instead: per query, bound to \verb{?},
or once through \verb{ALTER TABLE … ALTER COLUMN … SET DATA TYPE GEOMETRY USING}, which drops the CRS because it names the bare type.
\item \strong{An \code{sf} or \code{sfc} column is not written, and may not say so.}
A whole \code{sf} object handed to \code{dbWriteTable()} fails inside sf's own \code{dbDataType()} method,
which writes EWKB hex into a column DuckDB parses as WKT (\href{https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-r/issues/1670}{#1670});
a bare \code{sfc} column is worse: a \code{POINT} column writes \emph{silently} as \code{DOUBLE[]},
and other geometry types abort with a message naming neither column nor type.
Convert to text or to WKB first, and take one of the routes above;
a \code{wk_wkb} column is no shortcut, because it writes as \code{BLOB} like any other list of raw vectors, dropping its class and its CRS.
The duckspatial and duckdbfs packages wrap this.
What to do about the write side is \href{https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-r/blob/main/plan/PLAN-spatial-interop.md}{\code{plan/PLAN-spatial-interop.md}}
(\href{https://github.com/duckdb/duckdb-r/issues/117}{#117}).
\item \strong{The \code{spatial} extension's own types are aliases, and read as what they alias.}
\code{POINT_2D}, \code{POINT_3D}, \code{POINT_4D}, \code{BOX_2D} and \code{BOX_2DF} are structs, and read as data frame columns;
\code{LINESTRING_2D} and \code{LINESTRING_3D} are lists of point structs, and read as lists of data frames;
\code{POLYGON_2D} and \code{POLYGON_3D} are lists of those rings, and read as lists of lists;
\code{WKB_BLOB} is a \code{BLOB}, and reads as raw vectors.
The same shapes write the plain struct or list, and \code{field.types} naming the alias casts back to it.
WKT does not parse into them, but they cast to and from \code{GEOMETRY} in the query, as \verb{'POINT (1 2)'::GEOMETRY::POINT_2D}.
Arrow carries the storage without the alias.
